---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Photo from '../components/Photo.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { contact } from '../data/site';

import heroImg from '../assets/images/eggs-in-wooden-bowl.jpg';
---
<Base
  title="About Orchard — A Farm in San José del Cabo"
  description="Orchard is a small poultry farm in the Buenos Aires neighbourhood of San José del Cabo, producing eggs and poultry for Los Cabos — and open to visitors."
  breadcrumb={[{ name: 'About', href: '/about/' }]}
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="About"
    title="A small farm under old mango trees."
    lead="Orchard is a poultry farm on the Buenos Aires side of San José del Cabo — close enough to the airport to be improbable, and quiet enough that you forget it."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap ab">
      <div class="ab__media">
        <Photo src={heroImg}
          alt="Freshly collected Orchard eggs in a wooden bowl, in the grass beneath the mango trees."
          sizes="(min-width: 900px) 42vw, 100vw" widths={[420, 640, 900, 1100]} ratio="4/5" priority />
      </div>
      <div class="prose ab__body">
        <p>
          Baja California Sur is dry country. Most of what gets eaten in Los Cabos arrives
          from somewhere else — up the peninsula, across the water, or from further away
          than that. By the time it reaches a kitchen here it has been travelling for days.
        </p>
        <p>
          Orchard started from a simple objection to that. There is a stand of eighty-year-old
          mango trees on the edge of San José del Cabo, and underneath them the air moves and
          the ground stays in shade. It is a good place to keep birds. So we do.
        </p>

        <h2>What we actually do</h2>
        <p>
          We raise laying hens and poultry outdoors under those trees. We formulate and mill
          our own feed on the farm rather than buying it in — corn, wheat bran, soybean meal
          and scratch grains, with ginger, turmeric, garlic, onion and oregano — because it
          is the one input that changes everything downstream and we would rather control it.
          No hormones are added, and nothing artificial goes into the mix.
        </p>
        <p>
          Eggs are collected by hand every morning, sorted, packed and delivered across
          Los Cabos by us. The shells come out brown, cream, freckled and pale blue-green
          depending on the bird. We do not sort that out. It is the most honest thing about
          the product.
        </p>

        <h2>On the word "organic"</h2>
        <p>
          This site used to describe Orchard as organic. We have taken that word off it. We
          do not hold organic certification, and using the word without it is a claim we
          cannot stand behind — so we have replaced it with the things we can: where the
          birds live, what they eat, who makes their feed, and how quickly the eggs reach you.
        </p>
        <p>
          If those things matter to you, they are all verifiable. You can come and check.
        </p>

        <h2>Why the farm is open</h2>
        <p>
          Most children in Los Cabos have never seen a laying hen. Most adults have not
          either. We started running visits because people kept asking, and it turned into
          one of the more worthwhile things the farm does — school groups, families,
          birthday mornings, chefs who want to see the flock before they buy from it.
        </p>
        <p>
          A farm you can walk onto is a different kind of supplier. It is harder to run that
          way, and it is the whole point.
        </p>

        <h2>Where we are going</h2>
        <p>
          More birds, more colours in the carton, and more kitchens in Los Cabos serving eggs
          that were under a mango tree earlier the same week. We would also like more
          properties producing on their own land, with our help — the short chain works
          better the more of it there is.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">The farm, in brief</span>
      <dl class="ataglance">
        <div><dt>Where</dt><dd>{contact.areaShort}, {contact.region}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>What we raise</dt><dd>Laying hens, broilers and ornamental breeds</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Feed</dt><dd>Formulated and milled on the farm</dd></div>
        <div><dt>We serve</dt><dd>Households, hotels, restaurants, villas and private chefs across Los Cabos</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Open to visitors</dt><dd>Families and school groups, by appointment</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand location="about" />
</Base>

<style>
  .ab { display: grid; gap: clamp(2rem, 1rem + 4vw, 4rem); align-items: start; }
  .ab__body h2 { font-size: var(--t-h3); }
  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .ab { grid-template-columns: 0.85fr 1fr; }
    .ab__media { position: sticky; top: 100px; }
  }
  .ataglance { margin: 2rem 0 0; display: grid; gap: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .ataglance > div { display: grid; gap: 0.3rem 2rem; padding-block: 1.15rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .ataglance dt { font-size: var(--t-micro); letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); }
  .ataglance dd { margin: 0; }
  @media (min-width: 760px) { .ataglance > div { grid-template-columns: 14rem 1fr; align-items: baseline; } }
</style>
